//
// `@FR-N-Domain`'s GUARD licence, third family: the DIVISOR.
//
// The rule promises one elision — "provably in-domain (constant / range / GUARD)" — over every
// partial operation it names, and it names three families: `÷0`/`%0`, the domain-partial float
// functions, and `v[i]` OOB.  Walked as a RULE rather than as a site, the promise turned out to
// be kept unevenly, and the divisor's row was the INDEX's row one family over:
//
//     if d == 0 { return -1; }
//     x = a / d;                  // in-domain, and reported nullable
//
// The fact was already being computed.  `divisor_proof_from_condition` answers "proven on the
// ELSE side" for `d == 0` — the shape a guard clause needs — and nothing consumed it on the
// fall-through.  The index half got its guard-clause twin the same day, mirroring the null
// model's own (loft#585); this is the divisor taking the same one.
//
// ⚠ The truthy spelling is NOT part of this, and finding out why is the other half of the
// lesson.  `if !d { return … } … a / d` looks like the same guard without the zero, and an arm
// for it was written and reverted within the hour: in loft an integer condition is always TRUE
// (measured — `if 0 { … }` takes the THEN branch, and `!0` is `false` exactly as `!3` is), so
// that guard never fires and eliding on it made `f(10, 0)` answer a silent null through a
// non-null slot.  A widening that REMOVES a diagnostic has to be measured on the cell the
// diagnostic was about, not only on the cells where it was noise — and the cell that caught it
// is the refusal assertion below, which is why this file asserts both directions.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  the guard's spelling (positive · early return), and
//                        which fact the guard establishes (the right var · the wrong var · the
//                        wrong side · a body that does not diverge).
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   integer division; the float twin rides the same proof.
//
// @falsified-at: 023afd963 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0.  MEASURED both backends.
//
//   The EXIT channel moves on `cast_through_guard`: on the control the guarded division types
//   `integer?`, `(N-Cast)` refuses a possibly-null subject, and the file does not compile.  The
//   value cells are inert against that control by construction — the values were always right,
//   and a too-conservative type is invisible to a value assertion.
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1450g-the-divisor-guard-is-read-in-every-spelling.patch
//   — the same channels as the control above.  That control is reachable from no public
//   ref, so the patch is the runnable form of this receipt: it reintroduces the fault on
//   top of HEAD.  Score it with `scripts/falsify.sh <this file> --patch <that patch>`.
//   The channels matching are what say the patch reintroduces THIS defect, not a neighbour.
//   HOLDS: the assertion channel does NOT move — the cells read the same on both
//   trees — along with leak, panic, free-refusal and expectations.  The exit channel
//   carries this one, so a patch that moves an assertion is reintroducing something
//   else and must be rejected.

// The spelling that already worked — the control that says nothing was traded away.
fn positive(a: integer, d: integer) -> integer {
  if d != 0 { return a / d; }
  -1
}

// The guard-clause spelling.
fn early(a: integer, d: integer) -> integer {
  if d == 0 { return -1; }
  a / d
}

// The cell that carries the FALSIFICATION.  Every assertion below asserts a VALUE, and the
// values were always right — a too-conservative type is invisible to a value assertion.  A CAST
// reads it: `(N-Cast)` is an assertion and refuses a possibly-null subject, so this is a hard
// ERROR before the fix and the whole file fails to compile.
fn cast_through_guard(a: integer, d: integer) -> integer {
  if d == 0 { return -1; }
  ((a / d) as float * 2.0) as integer
}

fn main() {
  assert(positive(10, 2) == 5, "the positive guard divides");
  assert(early(10, 2) == 5, "the guard clause divides");
  assert(cast_through_guard(10, 2) == 10, "a cast through a guarded divisor compiles");

  // ---- the guards still turn away what they guard against ----
  assert(positive(10, 0) == -1, "the positive guard still refuses zero");
  assert(early(10, 0) == -1, "the guard clause still refuses zero");

  // ---- and the runtime is unchanged where the contract IS broken: `÷0` is still the C80 null,
  //      never a fault, which is what makes trusting a guarded divisor safe ----
  z = 0;
  q: integer = (10 / z) ?? -9;
  assert(q == -9, "an unguarded ÷0 is still the reserved null: {q}");

  println("1450g ok");
}
